<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1358">1358</a>–<a href="/years/1408">1408</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu was the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> of the Ashikaga shogunate, from 1368 to 1394. He reunited the Northern and Southern Courts in 1392, curbed powerful provincial lords such as the Ōuchi in 1399 and opened official trade with Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinkaku-ji">Golden Pavilion</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and patronised Noh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu.jpg" alt="Ashikaga Yoshimitsu" width="320" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yoshimitsu_Ashikaga_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ashikaga Yoshimitsu was born on 25 September 1358, the third son of Ashikaga Yoshiakira but the oldest to survive. His childhood name was Haruō. He was appointed shōgun in 1368 at the age of ten, the same year that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-chokei">Emperor Chōkei</a> ascended the southern throne, and at twenty he was admitted to the imperial court as Acting Grand Counselor.</p><p>In 1379 he reorganised the institutions of the Gozan Zen establishment, and two years later he became the first person of the warrior class to host a reigning emperor at his private residence. In 1378 he built the Muromachi palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, from whose location the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> and the period take their name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Yoshimitsu's chief political achievement was ending the fighting of the Nanboku-chō period. In 1392 he persuaded Go-Kameyama of the Southern Court to hand the Imperial Regalia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-komatsu">Emperor Go-Komatsu</a> of the Northern Court, which firmly established the authority of the Muromachi shogunate and curbed regional daimyō. The commander Imagawa Sadayo was sent to subdue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> in 1370, and the island was pacified by 1389.</p><p>In 1394 he gave the post of shōgun to his son Ashikaga Yoshimochi and became Grand Chancellor of State, the highest-ranking member of the court, but kept authority over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> until his death. In 1395 he retired from all offices, took the tonsure and moved to his Kitayama villa, which had a pavilion two-thirds covered in gold leaf.</p><p>At Kitayama he received envoys from the Ming and Joseon courts at least six times. He accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">tributary relations</a> with Ming China in 1401, and in 1402 or 1404 the title King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, bowing three times to read a Ming document addressing him so. Many modern Japanese historians view this negatively, and Tsuji Zennosuke and Kuroita Katsumi called it a violation of Japan's tradition. His son Yoshimochi ended the embassies in 1411. Arai Hakuseki, by contrast, thought the title proper because the shōgun was the de facto ruler. In 1407 he began a plan to become Daijō tennō, a title of retired emperors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Yoshimitsu died suddenly in 1408, aged 49, before the plan could be realised. His villa became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinkaku-ji">Rokuon-ji</a>, often called Kinkaku-ji, the Temple of the Golden Pavilion, where a statue of him stands. He was also the patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeami-motokiyo">Zeami Motokiyo</a>, regarded as the founder of Noh theatre, whom he encouraged to give the art a loftier treatment. His posthumous name was Rokuon'in.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ashikaga Yoshimitsu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name Muromachi comes from the Muromachi district of Kyoto, where the third shōgun, <b>Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</b>, built his residence known as the &quot;Flower Palace&quot; in 1379.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1392 <b>Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</b> negotiated reunification: the Southern emperor handed the regalia to Go-Komatsu, ending the schism.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-court">Northern Court</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 14th century Ouchi Yoshihiro held six provinces and controlled trade with Korea, but he rebelled against <b>Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</b> and died in 1399; his successors rebuilt the family's power across Suo, Nagato and northern Kyushu from about 1400.</blockquote><a href="/state/ouchi-clan-domain">Ouchi clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kyōto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinkaku-ji">Kinkaku-ji Temple</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1390" aria-label="Kyōto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7991 897 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ashikaga Yoshimitsu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8441 1167h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8441 1167h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1390">What was here in 1390? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
